<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gruffydd ap Llywelyn</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1010–1063</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Gruffudd ap Llywelyn</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gruffydd ap Llywelyn was king of Gwynedd and Powys from 1039 and, from about 1055, ruler of nearly all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, the only king ever to unite it. He defeated English and Welsh rivals, raided across the border with Mercian allies and took lands east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offas-dyke">Offa's Dyke</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-godwinson">Harold Godwinson</a>'s campaign of 1063 broke his power, and he was killed by his own men.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gruffydd-ap-llywelyn.jpg" alt="Gruffydd ap Llywelyn" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Powell and Humphrey Lloyd · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GruffuddapLlywelyn_historieofcambria.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
